{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at 004954dfe89b907e77737f6e5ac631f8d27db7c5. All 25 artifacts reviewed. Signature/Artifacts/SQLite feasibility and the recorded sequential measurements are supported. The restricted evaluator needs two corrections before the safety conclusion can be adopted.\n\nP1.1 — Cached JSONata expressions share mutable evaluation hooks (src/profile.ts:161–164, 214–271). compile returns the same expression object from a module cache. Each evaluate assigns entry/exit hooks into that expression, then awaits expression.evaluate. Concurrent evaluations overwrite the other call's hooks, active-depth map and budget counters. Exact-source repro at /tmp/artroom-checker-roomcore/spikes/room-core/.checker-concurrency.mjs: $count(changed[$glob($, $$.scope)]) on 80 paths returned value 80 with 484 steps/89894 inspected bytes sequentially; two simultaneous guarded calls produced one success at 1 step/0 bytes and one engine_error (undefined.count). Concurrent guarded plus guard:false produced two successes with the guarded call at 1 step/0 bytes. Bench methods are outside the act queue, so current RoomSpike can encounter this even though ordinary acts serialize per room. Isolate expression environment/hooks per invocation, or safely serialize all reuse of each shared expression including benchmark modes. Add concurrent guarded/guarded and guarded/unguarded regression cases asserting the same isolated outputs, counts, budget failures and recovery after an error as sequential evaluation. Preserve admitted-AST checks. Record this fourth requirement in the note; sequential replay does not demonstrate concurrent budget integrity. Lane C must not inherit mutable shared hooks.\n\nP1.2 — Deterministic require failures silently remove obligations; runtime faults also become recorded domain outcomes (src/room.ts:95–108, 161–166; profile.ts final catch). run wraps every ProfileError as {error,...}. The require loop ignores that value and records the proposal without the failed rule's obligation. Exact RoomSpike flow with real evaluator and only storage/auth/Artifacts stubbed reproduces a require expression that doubles a string before returning [{review:\"@security\"}]: it trips value_bytes, yet propose is appended with obligations:[]. Repro: /tmp/artroom-checker-roomcore/spikes/room-core/.checker-require-failure.mjs. Deterministic budget/type failures in require must refuse, with recorded rule/input/error, rather than weaken requirements. Separately, engine_error/unexpected engine faults must propagate as retryable infrastructure failure with no act/refusal/evaluation writes; atseq's runtime-profile.md expressly separates these faults from deterministic interpretation failures. The current broad conversion records a scheduling-dependent hook fault as durable policy evidence. Add failure-path tests proving a failed require cannot append a proposal and an injected unexpected engine fault records nothing, while genuine budget refusals remain deterministic and replayable. These are evaluator/admission fixes; a complete Room implementation is not required.\n\nArchitecture: the spike demonstrates the intended service mix and explains where the cost lies. Keeping immutable tree objects cached and capping readTree concurrency is supported by 50-run comparisons. The memoized frozen-input accounting is promising, but the supported safety claim must include invocation isolation and correct failure semantics. Aggregate per-act budgets and charged host-function work remain planned follow-on requirements. If budget/profile behavior changes, pin/version it and retain the evaluated inputs/usage. The proposed 75–225 ms figure is an estimate from measured per-step costs, not a proven worst-case CPU bound.\n\nSecurity: valid signatures are verified, but the spike intentionally lacks production room/domain binding, roster authorization, secret scanning and setup authentication; the Worker was deleted after measurement. Its simple nonce replay is not the production byte-mismatch contract. Do not promote those transport/admission parts unchanged. No literal Artifacts tokens found in recorded result files. No checker access to real credentials, live deployment or remote mutations.\n\nSimplification: a portable restricted evaluator with owned inputs and work-based limits is preferable to depending on frozen wall-clock timers. Preserve one bounded semantics across hosts, an explicit runtime-fault boundary and a small cache with safe lifecycle. Keep the tree-diff prototype; rename detection, depth/entry limits, cache limits and constrained globs remain Lane B/product work already in scope. No functionality cut is requested.\n\nValidation: npm ci with scripts disabled; wrangler types plus npm run check passed; Wrangler deploy --dry-run bundled 284.85 KiB. Independently exercised tree changes including edits/deletes/adds, file↔directory transitions, mode-only change, equal-tree skipping and cached zero-read reuse. Checked all result files, reported 50-run group sizes, 400 act timings, 301 accepted-act replays/1051 evaluations/no recorded difference, guard-mode output agreement and CPU-reset trace evidence. The committed main driver files contain summaries rather than individual timing samples, so distributions cannot be independently recomputed; their tables match the committed summarizer and group counts. Both failure repros above run against this exact source and expose cases absent from those sequential measurements.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c35e0befaccdc764b6e67d07711998225d0225a7","binding":"assigned","head":"004954dfe89b907e77737f6e5ac631f8d27db7c5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:77162946e696ed1242a5acf741afafd94564d0af\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d57a1e435880089431dac5d22dde1783032aab42\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361